  <subsection xml:id="subsec-190-Course_Policies">
    <title>Course Policies</title>
    
    <paragraphs>
      <title>Extra Credit</title>
      <p>
        There will be no extra credit available in this course. If you are interested in raising your grade, take the time you would have spent on 
        an extra credit assignment and spend it making sure you get more "regular credit" instead: 
        master a topic you've found difficult, find exercises in the textbook to work on, spend a few extra hours studying, etc.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Email/Messages</title>
      
      <p>
        I try to respond to emails/MOM messages within 24 hours -- typically, emails/messages received by 4:00pm will receive a response the same day, 
        and those received after 4:00pm will receive a response the following day. 
        You are encouraged to email/message me regarding any issues you are having in the course at any time; 
        however, emails/messages sent within 24 hours of a due date may not get a response before the deadline. 
        So, please, plan accordingly!
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Studying</title>
      
        <p>
          To succeed in this course, you will need to dedicate a significant amount of time to working on precalculus. 
          Mathematics is learned by doing, meaning it is important to work numerous problems to fully understand the concepts we cover. 
          The assigned work is the minimum amount you should be completing; if you feel like you don't fully understand a concept, 
          ask me for some additional practice problems. 
          This is a 4-credit course, which means there should be 4 learning hours per week. 
          A general rule (in all college courses) is for each learning hour per week you spend a minimum of two additional hours on problem sets, 
          studying, reading the text, etc. Since this course is 4-credits, you should plan to spend at least an additional 8 hours 
          (for a total of at least 12 hours) working on precalculus per week.
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Tutoring</title>
        <p>
            The WSU Math Center (Sullivan 140) will be offering free drop-in tutoring for this course this semester. 
            Details will be announced during the first week of class.
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Accomodations</title>
      
      <p>
        If you anticipate issues related to the format or requirements of this course, please contact me as early as possible to discuss ways to 
        ensure your full participation. If you believe formal, disability-related accommodations are appropriate, it is very important you register 
        with the Student Accessibility Services office and notify me of your eligibility for such accommodations.
        We can then plan how to best coordinate your accommodations.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Academic Integrity and AI Usage</title>

      <p>
        The WSU Mathematics Department does not tolerate academic dishonesty! For an explanation of what is considered "academic dishonesty" at 
        WSU, please see the policy included in the <url href="https://catalog.worcester.edu/undergraduate/academic-policies-procedures/academic-honesty">2025-2026 
        Undergraduate Catalog</url>. All instructors in the WSU Mathematics Department are required to 
        report all incidents of academic dishonesty observed, detected, or otherwise determined to have occurred to the WSU Academic Central File. 
        WSU requires some penalty be imposed for every reported violation of the academic honesty policy. All incidents of academic dishonesty in 
        this course will result in (at least) a score of 0 on that assignment; additional penalties will be imposed at the instructor's discretion 
        and/or as required by the 
        <url href="https://webcdn.worcester.edu/mathematics/wp-content/uploads/sites/17/2023/03/Math-Department-Academic-Honesty-Policy.pdf">Mathematics 
        Department Academic Honesty Policy</url>. In this course, instances of academic dishonesty will include (but not limited to!):
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Submitting any assignment you did not complete yourself, either work produced by a classmate/friend or from an online resources 
            (such as an online calculator) or third party (such as Chegg, Course Hero, etc.).
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Using course materials/notes, the internet, or a calculator/other device in any unauthorized way during an online quiz or an in-person exam.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Sharing the contents of a quiz with a classmate.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            AI cannot be used on graded assignments in this course unless otherwise specified.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Instructor Rights</title>
      <p>
        <alert> As the instructor of this course, I reserve the right to make changes to the above course policies at any time. All changes will be announced in class.</alert>
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-190-StudentLearningOutcomes">
    <title>Student Learning Outcomes</title>
    <p>
      Students will be able to
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          Justify their mathematical reasoning and use correct mathematical notation and terminology.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Solve exponential, logarithmic, and trigonometric equations, as well as systems of linear equations.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        Analyze and transform graphs of exponential, logarithmic, and trigonometric functions.
      </li>
      <li>
        Simplify and evaluate expressions involving logarithms, trigonometric functions, and inverse trigonometric functions.
      </li>
      <li>
        Prove and apply trigonometric identities, including those involving the sums/differences of angles, half angles, and double angles.
      </li>
      <li>
        Solve problems from real life applications such as growth and decay, triangle measurement, or harmonic motion.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-190-LASC">
    <title>LASC</title>
    <p>
       This is a Quantitative Reasoning (QR) course in the Liberal Arts and Sciences Curriculum (LASC). 
    </p>
    <p>
        The course addresses the following LASC Quantitative Reasoning objectives:
    </p>
    <ol>
        <li>
            <p>
                Acquaint students with formal systems, procedures, and sequences of operations.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Strengthen students' understanding of variables and functions.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Apply mathematical techniques to the analysis and solution of real-life problems.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Emphasize the importance of accuracy, including precise language and careful definitions of mathematical concepts.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Understand both the underlying principles and practical applications of one or more fields of mathematics.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
        and the following LASC overarching objectives:
    </p>
    <ol>
        <li>
            <p>
                Communicate effectively orally and in writing.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Understand and employ quantitative and qualitative reasoning.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Apply skills in critical thinking.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Understand the roles of science and technology in our modern world.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Make connections across courses and disciplines.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Develop as healthy individuals--physically, emotionally, socially, ethically, and intellectually.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ol>
  </subsection>

